  "query_preview": "(This evening through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 202 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nOverview.\n\nCentral Indiana is set for a prolonged stretch of cool, \ndry autumn weather. High pressure anchored to the north and \nnortheast will keep a continuous supply of cool air moving into the \nregion on northeasterly winds. Persistent low level moisture \ncombined with cooler air will keep skies mostly cloudy to cloudy \nthrough Thursday. Skies will then clear Friday into the weekend with \na slight warming trend going into next week.\n\nTonight Through Thursday.\n\nBroad high pressure continues to remain locked in place across the \nGreat Lakes region along with low pressure to the southeast allowing \nfor persistent northeasterly flow both at the surface and near \nsurface. This is helping to keep widespread cloud cover in place \nacross much of central Indiana along with keeping temperatures \nslightly below normal. A weak shortwave will push southeast across \nIowa tonight into tomorrow which will interact with a remnant vort \nmax across Ohio during the daytime hours to bring few light rain \nshowers to central Indiana, mainly across the eastern counties. \nBeyond those low chances, dry weather is expected through the \nremainder of the 7 day forecast with a fairly steady-state weather \npattern continuing into Thursday.\n\nFriday Through Tuesday.\n\nIncreasing subsidence aloft along with weak upper level flow will \nbegin to break down the cloud cover Friday into the weekend will \nhelp to bring temperatures closer to normal for this time of the \nyear. Min RH values will drop below 40 percent across south central \nIndiana which combined with the drier conditions of late, may need \nto be monitored for a low-end fire danger. Near calm winds will help \nto limit that threat.\n\nLooking further into early next week, temperatures in the lower 80s \nlook to return as the surface flow gradually gains a more southerly \ncomponent along with gradually increasing rain chances towards late \nTuesday ahead of the next system.",
